# Release Management

## Objective

Respect the project's existing versioning scheme and changelog conventions when a change is release-relevant.

## Core Principles

- Follow the project's existing versioning scheme (e.g. SemVer) when a change affects the public API/behavior.
- Update the changelog for user-facing changes, matching the existing changelog format.
- Flag breaking changes explicitly so they're versioned/released correctly, not bundled silently into a patch release.
- Keep release-relevant changes isolated and reviewable, not mixed with unrelated refactors.

## Requirements

- Add a changelog entry for any user-facing fix/feature, in the project's existing format.
- Classify a change correctly as patch/minor/major based on actual impact, especially for breaking changes.
- Avoid bundling an unrelated refactor into a change meant to be a small, reviewable patch release.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Introducing a breaking API change without flagging it as a major version bump.
- Making a user-facing fix with no changelog entry.
- Mixing an unrelated large refactor into what should be a small isolated fix.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Changelog is updated for user-facing changes.
✓ Version impact (patch/minor/major) matches the actual nature of the change.
✓ Release-relevant changes stayed focused, without unrelated bundled refactors.

## Golden Rule

Someone reading only the changelog should understand what changed and whether it's safe to upgrade.
